1. Install the nfs-kernel-server package on your Linux VM and create a shared directory named /srv/music_share for sharing music files.
=>apt-get install -y nfs-kernel-server nfs-common
mkdir -p /srv/music_share
chmod 777 /srv/music_share

2. Configure /etc/exports to share /srv/music_share with read and write permissions to a specific client IP (use your own second VM or localhost for testing), then restart the NFS service.
=>echo "/srv/music_share 127.0.0.1(rw,sync,no_subtree_check,no_root_squash)" > /etc/exports
exportfs -ra
Result of exportfs -v:
text/srv/music_share  127.0.0.1(sync,...,rw,no_root_squash,...)

3. On a client machine, mount the NFS share from your server to a local directory named ~/mnt/playlist and verify you can create and access files in the shared folder.
=>mkdir -p ~/mnt/playlist
mount -t nfs 127.0.0.1:/srv/music_share ~/mnt/playlist
Observed error (expected in this environment):
textmount.nfs: No such device for 127.0.0.1:/srv/music_share on /root/mnt/playlist

4. Simulate a Zomato-style shared menu folder: add a subfolder called 'restaurant_menus' inside your NFS share, copy any 2 PDF or image files into it, and confirm they are visible from the client mount.
=>mkdir -p /srv/music_share/restaurant_menus
echo "sample menu 1" > /srv/music_share/restaurant_menus/menu1.pdf
echo "sample menu 2" > /srv/music_share/restaurant_menus/menu2.jpg
Contents of the share:
text/srv/music_share/
└── restaurant_menus/
    ├── menu1.pdf
    └── menu2.jpg
These files would be visible on the client mount point once NFS is functional.

5. Un-mount the NFS share from the client and update your /etc/exports file to restrict access to read-only, then remount and test that you cannot create or modify files in the shared folder.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use the 'ro' option in /etc/exports for read-only access.</em>
=>umount ~/mnt/playlist
Update /etc/exports to read-only:
Bashecho "/srv/music_share 127.0.0.1(ro,sync,no_subtree_check,no_root_squash)" > /etc/exports
exportfs -ra
Verified export:
text/srv/music_share  127.0.0.1(...,ro,...)
After remounting on a working NFS system, write attempts (touch, echo > file, etc.) would fail with “Read-only file system”, confirming the ro restriction.
